WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:03.680
A pump set: pump, coupling and electric motor.

2
00:00:04.419 --> 00:00:09.503
Inside the motor the shaft runs on bearings, and every bearing fails one day.

3
00:00:10.208 --> 00:00:11.334
When do you step in?

4
00:00:12.067 --> 00:00:13.518
There are four strategies.

5
00:00:14.543 --> 00:00:18.943
With reactive maintenance you run it until it breaks, and then you repair it.

6
00:00:19.744 --> 00:00:21.834
Fine when that is cheap and safe.

7
00:00:22.535 --> 00:00:25.565
As a default it is expensive: by the rule of thumb,

8
00:00:25.763 --> 00:00:29.304
an unplanned repair costs three to five times a planned one.

9
00:00:30.308 --> 00:00:33.663
With preventive maintenance you replace at a fixed interval,

10
00:00:33.756 --> 00:00:35.590
however good the part still is.

11
00:00:36.319 --> 00:00:38.931
That works for parts that clearly wear out.

12
00:00:40.029 --> 00:00:42.862
With condition-based maintenance you measure the condition,

13
00:00:42.989 --> 00:00:47.285
such as the vibration on the bearing housing, and act above an agreed limit.

14
00:00:48.321 --> 00:00:50.225
Predictive maintenance goes further:

15
00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:53.418
from the trend you forecast when the limit will be reached,

16
00:00:53.557 --> 00:00:57.388
and you plan the repair into a stop when the line is down anyway.

17
00:00:58.659 --> 00:01:02.061
Which strategy fits depends on what the failure costs.

18
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.963
Take the gearbox of the bottleneck press in a stamping plant: 60,000 euros new,

19
00:01:08.172 --> 00:01:09.600
and 10 weeks delivery.

20
00:01:10.602 --> 00:01:13.470
Running it to failure would stop the plant for weeks.

21
00:01:14.173 --> 00:01:17.958
A monthly vibration route spots bearing damage months ahead.

22
00:01:18.693 --> 00:01:20.086
So: condition-based.

23
00:01:21.115 --> 00:01:24.865
The conveyor motors on the packing line cost 400 euros,

24
00:01:24.993 --> 00:01:27.907
are in stock and are swapped in 15 minutes.

25
00:01:28.595 --> 00:01:29.988
Those you run to failure.

26
00:01:31.028 --> 00:01:35.510
Choosing like this, per asset and per failure mode, by the consequences,

27
00:01:35.753 --> 00:01:36.879
is called RCM.

28
00:01:37.569 --> 00:01:42.643
Good plants do 85 percent or more of their maintenance hours as planned work.

29
00:01:43.965 --> 00:01:45.788
How do parts actually fail?

30
00:01:46.524 --> 00:01:51.238
The textbook shows the bathtub curve: the failure rate plotted against age.

31
00:01:52.242 --> 00:01:55.435
First infant mortality: failures soon after fitting,

32
00:01:55.551 --> 00:01:57.710
from fitting errors or bad parts.

33
00:01:58.731 --> 00:02:03.433
Then a long flat period of random failure, the same chance at any age.

34
00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.707
And at the end wear-out.

35
00:02:06.763 --> 00:02:11.895
But in a well-known airline study only 11 percent of the parts wore out with age;

36
00:02:12.034 --> 00:02:14.089
68 percent failed early.

37
00:02:14.771 --> 00:02:18.335
What wears is mostly what rubs, cuts or carries load.

38
00:02:19.687 --> 00:02:25.643
Every 12 months, a packaging plant replaces the bearings in all 24 servo axes.

39
00:02:26.387 --> 00:02:29.081
That costs 18,000 euros a year.

40
00:02:30.106 --> 00:02:33.229
Before the programme there were 6 bearing failures a year,

41
00:02:33.414 --> 00:02:35.597
after it not fewer but more: 9.

42
00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:41.580
And 5 of those came within two weeks after a swap: maintenance-induced failure.

43
00:02:42.310 --> 00:02:44.400
The work itself causes the failure.

44
00:02:45.433 --> 00:02:49.415
Opening a machine can let dirt in or leave a seal badly fitted.

45
00:02:50.177 --> 00:02:53.892
You then see a cluster of failures just after the maintenance.

46
00:02:55.187 --> 00:02:59.181
You test the pattern with a Weibull analysis of the failure ages.

47
00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.922
The output is the Weibull beta, the shape factor.

48
00:03:03.924 --> 00:03:06.838
Beta below 1: early failure, fix the fitting.

49
00:03:07.640 --> 00:03:09.103
Around 1: random.

50
00:03:09.826 --> 00:03:15.410
Above 1, and especially above 3: wear-out, and then replacing on time makes sense.

51
00:03:16.399 --> 00:03:21.217
For the servo bearings the fit gives beta 1.0: random failure, not wear-out.

52
00:03:22.007 --> 00:03:23.981
So the yearly swap could never help.

53
00:03:25.028 --> 00:03:26.421
The plant stops the swap,

54
00:03:26.538 --> 00:03:31.194
checks the 6 critical axes for vibration every month and trains the fitters.

55
00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:33.716
Next year: 3 failures.

56
00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:38.074
A bearing defect starts as a pit in the raceway.

57
00:03:38.781 --> 00:03:43.750
At point P it becomes measurable; at point F the pump no longer does its job.

58
00:03:44.791 --> 00:03:47.311
The time in between is the P-F interval:

59
00:03:47.473 --> 00:03:50.295
your window to see the defect and plan the repair.

60
00:03:51.320 --> 00:03:55.372
So, as a rule of thumb, measure at half the window: at 8 weeks,

61
00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.125
at least every 4 weeks.

62
00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:01.401
With a window of 10 weeks and a route every 12 weeks,

63
00:04:01.564 --> 00:04:04.559
you are in time only 83 percent of the time.

64
00:04:05.850 --> 00:04:08.915
Each method sees the defect at its own moment.

65
00:04:09.691 --> 00:04:14.231
Ultrasound listens far above our hearing and hears a dry bearing first:

66
00:04:14.335 --> 00:04:16.285
about 12 weeks before F.

67
00:04:17.281 --> 00:04:21.867
Vibration analysis on the bearing housing sees it about 10 weeks ahead;

68
00:04:22.029 --> 00:04:24.224
each fault has its own frequency.

69
00:04:25.215 --> 00:04:29.174
In oil analysis a lab looks for wear metals in an oil sample:

70
00:04:29.313 --> 00:04:31.043
about 6 weeks before F.

71
00:04:32.032 --> 00:04:35.469
Thermography only sees it once the bearing runs warm,

72
00:04:35.597 --> 00:04:38.871
about 4 weeks ahead; a hand on the housing 1 week.

73
00:04:39.561 --> 00:04:42.197
So the P-F interval depends on the method.

74
00:04:43.530 --> 00:04:48.453
The monthly route on a cooling-water pump measures 1.8 millimetres per second,

75
00:04:48.674 --> 00:04:52.888
then 1.9, 2.6 and in April 3.4.

76
00:04:53.613 --> 00:04:56.237
A bearing frequency shows in the spectrum.

77
00:04:57.329 --> 00:05:00.185
That is about 0.8 more each month.

78
00:05:00.872 --> 00:05:03.090
The action level is 4.5.

79
00:05:04.090 --> 00:05:10.267
The gap to the limit, divided by 0.8, is 1.4 months: about six weeks.

80
00:05:10.952 --> 00:05:16.281
A new bearing costs 350 euros; the next planned stop is in mid-May.

81
00:05:17.272 --> 00:05:21.347
So the planner orders the bearing now and books four hours in that stop.

82
00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:24.338
No pump seizing on a Friday night.
